1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,667
A team operating a feature day to day genuinely needs fifteen panels: latency by

2
00:00:04,667 --> 00:00:08,930
percentile, cost by model, error rate by endpoint, every number 4.5 through 8.6

3
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:10,009
taught you to watch.

4
00:00:10,009 --> 00:00:13,517
That dashboard is doing its job correctly by being comprehensive.

5
00:00:13,517 --> 00:00:17,888
Hand that same dashboard to a leadership review and watch it fail completely, not

6
00:00:17,888 --> 00:00:22,151
because the numbers are wrong, but because comprehensive and decision-ready are

7
00:00:22,151 --> 00:00:23,230
different qualities.

8
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:25,335
Leadership isn't operating the feature.

9
00:00:25,335 --> 00:00:29,760
They're deciding whether to fund it, expand it, or pull it back, and that decision

10
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:31,972
needs three or four numbers, not fifteen.

11
00:00:31,972 --> 00:00:34,671
Which three would you actually defend in the room?

12
00:00:34,671 --> 00:00:36,559
This isn't a hypothetical mismatch.

13
00:00:36,559 --> 00:00:40,876
It's usually the first thing that goes wrong when a feature moves from a scrappy

14
00:00:40,876 --> 00:00:44,222
team project to something the rest of the company is watching.

15
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:48,053
The instinct is to show more, not less, because more feels more honest.

16
00:00:48,053 --> 00:00:52,317
It's usually the opposite: more panels just give leadership more places to find

17
00:00:52,317 --> 00:00:54,907
whatever story they already believed walking in.

18
00:00:54,907 --> 00:00:59,386
Three or four numbers force a discipline fifteen never will: you have to decide, in

19
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:02,731
advance, which ones actually matter enough to survive the cut.

20
00:01:02,731 --> 00:01:07,210
That decision is worth making deliberately, not by whichever panels happened to get

21
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:07,858
built first.

22
00:01:08,408 --> 00:01:11,930
Read the metrics row against 9.1, because it's not a coincidence.

23
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:16,319
The three metrics that lecture built specifically for a probabilistic feature are

24
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:20,707
exactly the ones dense enough in meaning to belong on a one-page leadership view.

25
00:01:20,707 --> 00:01:25,259
Everything else in your team's fifteen panels exists to help you diagnose why one of

26
00:01:25,259 --> 00:01:29,647
those three moved, not to be read by someone deciding whether to keep funding the

27
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:30,081
feature.

28
00:01:30,081 --> 00:01:33,223
Refresh cadence matters more than it looks like it should.

29
00:01:33,223 --> 00:01:37,612
A leadership dashboard refreshing every minute doesn't help anyone make a monthly

30
00:01:37,612 --> 00:01:41,459
decision, it just adds noise between the meetings that actually matter.

31
00:01:41,459 --> 00:01:45,631
Match the refresh rate to the decision cycle, not to what your infrastructure

32
00:01:45,631 --> 00:01:46,986
happens to be capable of.

33
00:01:46,986 --> 00:01:50,941
These two documents can share a source of truth without sharing a screen.

34
00:01:50,941 --> 00:01:53,379
The same instrumentation from 9.2 feeds both.

35
00:01:53,379 --> 00:01:57,443
The team's version queries it in detail, sliced by endpoint and percentile.

36
00:01:57,443 --> 00:02:01,777
The leadership version rolls the same underlying events up to three numbers: one

37
00:02:01,777 --> 00:02:03,240
pipeline, two honest views.

38
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:07,737
Ask which document you're actually looking at before trusting either one's silence.

39
00:02:07,737 --> 00:02:11,313
A quiet number on the team's dashboard might mean nothing's wrong.

40
00:02:11,313 --> 00:02:15,756
The same quiet number missing entirely from the leadership version might just mean

41
00:02:15,756 --> 00:02:19,820
it didn't make the cut, a different and much less reassuring kind of quiet.

42
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:24,763
Say the honest caveat, because the pressure on a leadership dashboard runs one

43
00:02:24,763 --> 00:02:28,480
direction: make it look good, quietly drop the panel that doesn't.

44
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:32,760
A trustworthy leadership dashboard has to be allowed to show a number that's

45
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:37,040
currently bad, with a sentence next to it saying what's being done about it.

46
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:41,658
The version that only ever shows green gets believed right up until the quarter it

47
00:02:41,658 --> 00:02:46,389
can't hide the real number any longer, and every green quarter before that one loses

48
00:02:46,389 --> 00:02:48,078
its credibility retroactively.

49
00:02:48,078 --> 00:02:52,753
This is the same discipline as 9.1's acceptance-versus-engagement pairing, aimed at

50
00:02:52,753 --> 00:02:55,118
an entire dashboard instead of one metric.

51
00:02:55,118 --> 00:02:59,398
A flattering number is easy to produce by choosing what to measure and when.

52
00:02:59,398 --> 00:03:03,735
A dashboard that's allowed to look bad occasionally is the only kind actually

53
00:03:03,735 --> 00:03:07,058
reporting reality rather than managing an impression of it.

54
00:03:07,058 --> 00:03:09,873
The instinct to omit rarely feels like dishonesty.

55
00:03:09,873 --> 00:03:14,323
It feels like editing: choosing the story worth fifteen minutes of a leadership

56
00:03:14,323 --> 00:03:14,773
meeting.

57
00:03:14,773 --> 00:03:19,279
The real test is whether the bad number would still make the cut if it moved the

58
00:03:19,279 --> 00:03:21,588
wrong way, not just when it's flattering.

59
00:03:21,588 --> 00:03:26,093
Naming it is where credibility actually gets built: a team that surfaces its own

60
00:03:26,093 --> 00:03:30,542
weak quarter, with a plan attached, earns more trust than one whose numbers are

61
00:03:30,542 --> 00:03:32,288
suspiciously smooth every time.

62
00:03:32,838 --> 00:03:37,254
That's the standard worth building toward, and the exercise usually lands quickly,

63
00:03:37,254 --> 00:03:41,455
because most people already know which panel got looked at and which ones were

64
00:03:41,455 --> 00:03:42,209
scrolled past.

65
00:03:42,209 --> 00:03:46,678
Cut to those three or four honestly, even if it means retiring a chart you're proud

66
00:03:46,678 --> 00:03:46,840
of.

67
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:49,856
A dashboard's value was never the count of panels on it.

68
00:03:49,856 --> 00:03:53,033
It was always whether the room left knowing what to decide.

69
00:03:53,033 --> 00:03:56,749
Do this exercise even if you weren't the one who built the dashboard.

70
00:03:56,749 --> 00:04:01,057
Most people sitting in a leadership review can tell you exactly which number the

71
00:04:01,057 --> 00:04:05,204
room actually reacted to, and it's almost always one of three or four, buried

72
00:04:05,204 --> 00:04:08,651
somewhere among a dozen others competing for the same attention.

73
00:04:09,201 --> 00:04:10,833
One thing before you move on.

74
00:04:10,833 --> 00:04:15,391
Cut the dashboard first: adoption, acceptance, deflection, and one cost or margin

75
00:04:15,391 --> 00:04:17,585
number from Section seven, on one page.

76
00:04:17,585 --> 00:04:19,386
Then check its history honestly.

77
00:04:19,386 --> 00:04:23,944
If every quarter has looked fine, that's worth a second look before you trust the

78
00:04:23,944 --> 00:04:26,251
next one, not a reason to feel reassured.

79
00:04:26,251 --> 00:04:28,390
Both of these are edits, not rebuilds.

80
00:04:28,390 --> 00:04:31,260
The data already exists from 9.2's instrumentation.

81
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:34,692
This is a smaller, more honest view of it, not a new project.

82
00:04:34,692 --> 00:04:39,138
Next lecture is this section's workshop: building the metric tree that connects

83
00:04:39,138 --> 00:04:43,640
every number this section has taught you into the single document your feature's

84
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:44,821
whole story lives in.
